  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Advantages of Unity With SU(4)-Color — As a tribute to Abdus Salam, I recall the initiation in 1972-73 of the idea of grand uniﬁcation based on the view that lepton number is the fourth color.\n\n**Advantages of Unity With SU(4)-Color**\n\n*Baryogenesis and Proton Decay1*\n\n73 pp, 24 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** As a tribute to Abdus Salam, I recall the initiation in 1972-73 of the idea of grand uniﬁcation based on the view that lepton number is the fourth color. Motivated by aesthetic demands, these attempts led to the suggestion that the existing SU(2)×U(1) symmetry be extended minimally to the quark-lepton and left-right symmetric non- Abelian gauge structure G(2, 2, 4) = SU(2)L × SU(2)R × SU(4)-color. This served to unify members of a family within a single L-R self-conjugate multiplet.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 1.1 Salam in Perspective; 1.2 Aspects of My Collaboration with Salam; 1.3 A Preview; 2 Status of Particle Physics in 1972: The Grow; 2.1 From SU(2) U(1) to G(2,2,4) and Beyond; 2.2 Advantages of the Standard Model; 2.3 Why Choose The Symmetry G(2,2,4)?; 2.4 The Unwanted Right-Handed Neutrino; 2.5 Left-Right Symmetry in the Fundamental Law; 2.6 Non-Conservations of B and L: Proton Decay; 2.7 Going Beyond G(2,2,4): SO(10) and SU(5); 3 Gauge Coupling Unification; 3.1 SUSY SO(10) Versus a String-Unified G(2,2,; 4 Breaking SO(10) or G(2,2,4) to the SM; 5 Masses and Mixings of Fermions in SO(10) or ; 6 Neutrino Masses Shedding Light on Unificatio",
